1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,620
How do noise-cancelling headphones make a noise disappear?

2
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:07,340
Surprisingly, they do it by adding more sound.

3
00:00:07,270 --> 00:00:09,340
Sound is a wave of pressure:

4
00:00:09,270 --> 00:00:13,260
the air gets squeezed, then stretched, over and over.

5
00:00:13,190 --> 00:00:17,900
A tiny microphone on the outside of each ear cup hears that wave coming in.

6
00:00:17,950 --> 00:00:20,700
Then a chip builds an upside-down copy:

7
00:00:20,630 --> 00:00:25,100
a stretch wherever the noise squeezes, and a squeeze wherever it stretches.

8
00:00:25,030 --> 00:00:27,820
The speaker plays that flipped wave into your ear,

9
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:30,220
and the two add up to almost nothing.

10
00:00:30,740 --> 00:00:33,730
Physicists call it destructive interference.

11
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:37,060
But the copy has to stay exactly in step.

12
00:00:36,990 --> 00:00:40,900
With a steady, low hum, like an engine's drone, that's easy.

13
00:00:40,870 --> 00:00:43,460
Its waves are metres long and keep repeating,

14
00:00:43,390 --> 00:00:45,540
so the chip has time to match them.

15
00:00:45,470 --> 00:00:47,780
A high-pitched squeak is harder.

16
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:50,100
Its waves are only a few centimetres long,

17
00:00:50,030 --> 00:00:53,220
so the tiniest delay knocks the copy out of step.

18
00:00:53,150 --> 00:00:55,380
A sudden clatter is harder still:

19
00:00:55,310 --> 00:00:57,900
it changes faster than the chip can follow.

20
00:00:57,790 --> 00:01:00,300
That's why the soft cushions still matter.

21
00:01:00,190 --> 00:01:03,753
They block the high, sharp sounds the chip can't catch.

22
00:01:04,057 --> 00:01:08,127
So that's the answer: your headphones erase a noise with its own wave,

23
00:01:08,003 --> 00:01:10,660
flipped upside down and played right on time.
